  "candidate_0118": {
    "reason": "The assertion claims that synaptic stabilization maintains the persistence and integrity of new connections. This is a well-established biological principle (e.g., via mechanisms like protein synthesis-dependent consolidation). The previously rejected counterexample (sensory deprivation) fails because it introduces an unstated extreme condition (lack of activity/input) rather than testing the mechanism under standard conditions where stabilization occurs. Under normal physiological conditions, once a synapse is stabilized, it maintains its connections; the failure in deprivation models is due to the absence of the necessary maintenance signals (activity), not a failure of the stabilization property itself. No valid counterexample exists within the scope of standard biological function.",
    "sound_as_stated": true,
    "counterexample": null
  }
